---
import wordmark from '../assets/wordmark.svg?raw';
import mark from '../assets/mark.svg?raw';
import themeLight from '../assets/theme-light.svg?raw';
import themeDark from '../assets/theme-dark.svg?raw';
import themeSystem from '../assets/theme-system.svg?raw';
import hamburger from '../assets/hamburger.svg?raw';
import navClose from '../assets/nav-close.svg?raw';
---

<nav class="fixed top-0 inset-x-0 z-50 backdrop-blur-sm bg-surface/80 border-b border-ink/10">
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
    <!--
      #34, criterion 1/2. wordmark.svg is inlined (set:html), not referenced by <img>: the
      fill value on its path inherits colour from context, and an <img>-referenced SVG is an
      isolated document where that inheritance resolves against the file's own starting value
      rather than the host page, so it would render black in both themes. Inlining lets the
      wrapper's text-ink set the CSS colour the fill inherits, giving both modes for free with
      no per-mode class (criterion 3). It also keeps the asset's own <title> element in the
      accessibility tree, which an <img> would instead expose only via a separately-written alt
      attribute, so the accessible name comes from the asset itself rather than being restated
      here (criterion 2). Height-constrained rather than width-constrained per criterion 4's own
      reasoning: the nav is text-sm and the viewBox is ~4.79:1, so a fixed height keeps it
      inside the bar's existing padding at every breakpoint without touching the theme control
      beside it.

      #37, criterion 5. mark.svg is inlined the same way and for the same "one styled unit"
      reason, even though its fill values are fixed brand colours: it deliberately does not
      inherit from context the way the wordmark's path does (criterion 4a: the mark must not
      invert). The two SVGs share this flex wrapper rather than the nav's own row, so their
      gap-2 stays smaller than the row's own gap-x-6 to the links/theme group regardless of
      viewport (criterion 1b).

      #37, criterion 2. The wordmark asset's own accessible-name element (see its #34 comment
      above) is already the nav's sole one; mark.svg carries no such element of its own
      (design's asset, confirmed), and aria-hidden="true" on its wrapper is the second, separate
      requirement the spec calls the belt to that asset's braces: omitting the element alone is
      not sufficient on its own.
    -->
    <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2">
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="inline-flex items-center h-5 [&>svg]:h-full [&>svg]:w-auto" set:html={mark} />
      <span class="inline-flex items-center h-5 text-ink [&>svg]:h-full [&>svg]:w-auto" set:html={wordmark} />
    </span>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-1">
      <!--
        #44, criteria 2/3/5/8. `<details>`/`<summary>`, native `[open]` as the single source of
        truth for the disclosure state -- still true even after decision 3 below: no
        aria-expanded (details/summary already exposes expanded/collapsed to assistive technology
        without it, and a hardcoded attribute would go stale on the very first toggle).

        Decision 3 (docs/specs/hamburger-nav.md) was SUPERSEDED 17/08/2026: `<details>` cannot
        close itself when a link inside it is activated, so Mark chose a second scripted
        exception to the no-client-JS rule rather than leave the menu open over the section just
        navigated to. data-nav-menu below exists for exactly one consumer, the closing script at
        the foot of this file -- named rather than dropped, unlike the first draft of this
        comment, because that script is now the stated reason for it.

        Three hooks, one element each, so a mismatch with design's CSS fails as "class not
        found" rather than a silent no-op:
          .nav-menu        the <details> itself. Every rule that positions the disclosure panel
                            (criterion 8, overlay not push) lives in design's global.css against
                            this class, not here -- no position/inset utilities added in this
                            markup, the styling rule plus the review ruling on #44.
          .nav-menu-toggle  the <summary>. Marker removal (list-style, -webkit-details-marker),
                            the tap-target affordance, and the visible/hidden state of the
                            hamburger itself are both design's: this class does double duty,
                            styled one way below the collapse width (visible disclosure trigger)
                            and hidden entirely at/above it, since criterion 2 requires the nav
                            above 586px to stay exactly as it was, hamburger included.
          .nav-menu-links   the <ul>. Hidden below 586px unless [open], forced to render
                            regardless of [open] at/above it -- the fix for the resize trap
                            (open the menu narrow, widen past the breakpoint, links must not
                            disappear) -- both directions are design's media query.

        Icon: design chose a state-swapped pair (docs/design/hamburger-nav.md, `4cb5207`/
        `b654141`) mirroring #43's own swap rather than one icon plus a script-set transform --
        hamburger.svg and nav-close.svg both stay in the DOM, design's CSS shows exactly one via
        .nav-menu[open] targeting .nav-menu-toggle-icon-open/-close off the native [open]
        attribute alone, nothing script-set to keep in sync, same shape as #43's
        group-data-[theme-state=...] but keyed off a native attribute rather than one this file
        writes. No `group` class on <details>: design's selectors key off .nav-menu[open]
        directly rather than Tailwind's group-open: variant, so it would be dead weight left
        over from before design's CSS landed.
      -->
      <details class="nav-menu" data-nav-menu>
        <summary class="nav-menu-toggle" aria-label="Menu">
          <span
            aria-hidden="true"
            class="nav-menu-toggle-icon nav-menu-toggle-icon-open"
            set:html={hamburger}
          />
          <span
            aria-hidden="true"
            class="nav-menu-toggle-icon nav-menu-toggle-icon-close"
            set:html={navClose}
          />
        </summary>
        <ul class="nav-menu-links flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-1 text-sm text-muted justify-end">
          <li><a href="#about" class="hover:text-ink transition-colors duration-150">About</a></li>
          <li><a href="#expertise" class="hover:text-ink transition-colors duration-150">Expertise</a></li>
          <li><a href="#work" class="hover:text-ink transition-colors duration-150">Work</a></li>
          <li><a href="#experience" class="hover:text-ink transition-colors duration-150">Experience</a></li>
          <li><a href="#contact" class="hover:text-ink transition-colors duration-150">Contact</a></li>
        </ul>
      </details>
      <!--
        #43. Single cycling icon button, replacing the three-way group #26 built. Per
        docs/design/theme-icon-button.md: state is carried entirely by which icon is showing,
        no ring, fill or accent anywhere on this control. That's a deliberate removal, not an
        oversight: a single button has no unpressed sibling to distinguish itself from, so the
        #26 fill-inversion problem (docs/specs/rebrand.md) has nothing left to reintroduce
        itself through. Icon colour is the same text-muted/hover:text-ink pair every nav link
        already uses, clearing the applicable 3:1 non-text floor by the same wide margin those
        already clear 4.5:1 by.

        All three icons are inlined (set:html, same #34/#37 reasoning) and stay in the DOM the
        whole time; group-data-[theme-state=...] shows the one matching the button's own
        data-theme-state, set by Base.astro's script on load and on every click. Each is
        aria-hidden and title-free (belt-and-braces, #37); the button's own aria-label (set in
        Base.astro, see its #43 comment) is the nav's sole accessible name for this control.

        Sizing: h-5 (20px) icon inside p-1.5 (6px) padding = 32x32px content-and-padding box,
        design's own figure. The border (border-ink/10) adds 1px each side on top of that,
        so the border-box a hit-target check actually measures (getBoundingClientRect,
        Playwright's boundingBox()) is 34x34px, 10px clear of the WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8 24px floor
        in every direction (criterion 4/8b's tap-target limb). Naming both because the two
        differ and only one is what an assertion sees: measure this control's box before
        assuming which figure any future check against it used.

        Named by transition, per docs/specs/nav-lockup.md's own convention, never
        upper/lower (that pair inverts depending on whether you order by pixel value or by row
        count, and this fleet drifted off the convention independently in two places before
        catching it). The 3-row to 2-row transition (528px) does not move with this control's
        own width, and that is not a coincidence to leave unexplained: below 528px the nav
        links themselves wrap onto a second internal line before this button ever shares a row
        with them (confirmed by reading the links row's own top-position count, separately
        from this button's), so 528px is purely a function of the links' width, never this
        button's. The button only enters the layout at the 2-row to 1-row transition (586px,
        was 716px pre-#43), the point where the links row and this button can first share one
        row -- which is exactly why that number, and only that number, moved with this
        control's width.
      -->
      <button
        type="button"
        data-theme-toggle
        data-theme-state="system"
        aria-label="Theme: System. Press for Light."
        class="group inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-ink/10 p-1.5 text-muted hover:text-ink transition-colors duration-150"
      >
        <span
          aria-hidden="true"
          class="hidden h-5 group-data-[theme-state=light]:block [&>svg]:h-full [&>svg]:w-auto"
          set:html={themeLight}
        />
        <span
          aria-hidden="true"
          class="hidden h-5 group-data-[theme-state=dark]:block [&>svg]:h-full [&>svg]:w-auto"
          set:html={themeDark}
        />
        <span
          aria-hidden="true"
          class="hidden h-5 group-data-[theme-state=system]:block [&>svg]:h-full [&>svg]:w-auto"
          set:html={themeSystem}
        />
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
</nav>

<script>
  // #44, decision 3 SUPERSEDED (docs/specs/hamburger-nav.md): closes the menu when a link
  // inside it is activated, which <details> cannot do on its own.
  //
  // Fence widened 18/11/2026 at Mark's request (accessibility audit S3,
  // docs/plans/2026-11-18-accessibility-audit.md in the monorepo). The menu now also closes on
  // Escape, when focus leaves it, and on a click outside it, so an open menu can no longer sit
  // over the links a keyboard user has just tabbed to. Escape is the only path that moves
  // focus, and only back to the toggle, so focus is never stranded inside a closed panel. Still
  // out of scope: a focus trap, ARIA syncing (nothing here sets aria-expanded, see the comment
  // on <details> above), animation control and persisted open state.
  //
  // Not is:inline in <head> and not blocking, unlike #43's theme script: that one has to run
  // before first paint or the wrong theme flashes, this one only reacts to user input, so an
  // ordinary Astro component script (deferred, module, runs once the DOM it queries exists)
  // is the correct non-blocking form and copying #43's pattern here would be wrong.
  //
  // Additive, not load-bearing: with this script absent or failed, <details> still opens and
  // closes on its own native semantics and every link stays reachable, criteria 4 and 5 hold
  // either way. This only improves what happens after a tap, a key press or a focus move.
  const menu = document.querySelector<HTMLDetailsElement>("[data-nav-menu]");
  if (menu) {
    const close = () => menu.removeAttribute("open");

    menu.querySelectorAll("a").forEach((link) => {
      link.addEventListener("click", close);
    });

    menu.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
      if (event.key !== "Escape" || !menu.open) return;
      close();
      menu.querySelector("summary")?.focus();
    });

    menu.addEventListener("focusout", (event) => {
      const next = event.relatedTarget;
      if (menu.open && next instanceof Node && !menu.contains(next)) close();
    });

    document.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
      const target = event.target;
      if (menu.open && target instanceof Node && !menu.contains(target)) close();
    });
  }
</script>
